#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171771 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e264be4-288b-5105-91aa-d19fb7b8f8d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8502" :question/problem "Sketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y-1$ at the points indicated in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/fa81fd3beb39de6abd0fa2d7b68f9194.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,1), (-2,1)$, and $(-2,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(2,1)}&=2(1)-1=1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(-2,1)}&=2(1)-1=1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(-2,2)}&=2(2)-1=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/1f61f2a6d4602e7660245b212c064045.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171772 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "75eea83f-0067-5aa1-a0f3-1c611ac4e949" :question/math-academy-id "e-8503" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3y-1$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point along the line $y = -1$, the slope of the slope field does not vary\n2. At every point along the line $x = 0$, the slope of the slope field varies\n3. At every point along the line $y = 0$, the slope field is horizontal" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y$, the slope does not vary with $x$.\n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,-1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,-1)} = 3(-1)-1 = -4$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-1)$ is equal to $-4$ for any $x$.\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = 3y-1$,\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ depends on $y$.\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,0)} = 3(0)-1=-1 \\neq 0$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is $-1$ for any $x$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/23f047df3d0fa3e0d11f9be81ec6d279.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171773 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9b15835-9882-5758-b5ee-924ba48f7efb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d0d9819-c6b4-5996-8fa8-15de909fc7a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8504" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/ce8ff84e785ad9edbbd56ef02d4e0ed8.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y$, the slope does not vary with $x$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/fe3aa4398d5f839f6c68b1b1a7fa3b5d.png)\n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/3ac23747a88c5d438abce91c471b6228.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c9b15835-9882-5758-b5ee-924ba48f7efb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd19e0b-4abc-5626-ae1a-2baf3a5e71e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171326} {:answer/id #uuid "baf3e64c-d109-514d-8e48-7975403e283f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171327} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3a7bfd-98cb-530b-9917-f65ad982c625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171328} {:answer/id #uuid "3674efb6-581e-5520-b053-11fcf50dd7f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171329} {:answer/id #uuid "63b6b3a0-9cf0-5f95-b67f-d06c23d6f01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63b6b3a0-9cf0-5f95-b67f-d06c23d6f01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-96167-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171330} :answer-field/id #uuid "8108440e-deab-55f3-8701-ae6640db3081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b60ad5cb-19eb-5974-b3b8-9f949ad75efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-96167" :question/problem "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(y)$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For an autonomous equation $y'=f(y)$, every point on a fixed horizontal line must have the same slope. The selected diagram has slopes that change as $x$ changes along a horizontal line; therefore it cannot represent $y'=f(y)$. The other diagrams keep the slopes constant across each horizontal row."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "555ed665-7dd7-5e0b-8c9d-39c0f31376d8" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0bf6389-7a83-550d-86a4-83f48b1bb6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171458} {:answer/id #uuid "e43868e2-ac80-53c7-9254-d3175c0550a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171459} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c119b9-6d02-5deb-819c-543487e56ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171460} {:answer/id #uuid "033f4773-15ea-511a-96b1-2f8ae3e476de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171461} {:answer/id #uuid "23f44b99-717a-5dda-ba79-3aa361bec2de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171462}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5c119b9-6d02-5deb-819c-543487e56ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3351/Source/Images/q-97622-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171460} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f8f968-b2bd-5bc9-8e29-db075f5ee44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63ce3b5c-8d44-5ee9-a6b1-5ccb563ad615" :question/math-academy-id "q-97622" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{2} - y$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Use $y'=y^2-y$. At the two points with $y=1$, the slope is $1-1=0$, so both segments are horizontal. At the point with $y=-2$, the slope is $4+2=6$, a steep positive slope. Select the diagram with those three slopes."}]}
